            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Chorizo is a Uruguayan variant of the Spanish card game Escoba, featuring additional ways to capture cards and bonus scores for declaring combinations of cards in hand. The game is known for its lively, unpredictable nature with sudden changes of fortune. Some variants replace certain combinations with BÃ¡ciga rules.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Uruguay\nTime Period: 20th century\nHistorical Development: Evolved from the Spanish game Escoba, brought to Uruguay by European immigrants. The game developed unique local characteristics, particularly the declaration bonuses and wild card mechanics.\nCultural Significance: Popular in Uruguay, especially in Montevideo and Flores. Some players simply call it Escoba.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 4\nOptimal Players: 2 or 4 (partnerships)\nPartnerships: Yes for 4 players (partners opposite)',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Spanish 40-card deck\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included\nCard Values: Ace=1, 2-7=face value, Sota (Jack)=8, Caballo (Horse)=9, Rey (King)=10',
              },
              {
